1
00:00:00,440 --> 00:00:03,060
Hey, this Severs' guys' day out was one great idea.

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:04,820
Yeah, just four guys out on the town.

3
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:06,120
Eating anything we want.

4
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:08,660
Walking past discount jewelers without missing a beat.

5
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:12,760
Hey, you guys remember last month when Mom and Carol came to that bulls game?

6
00:00:13,020 --> 00:00:14,180
Oh, yeah. Remember what she said?

7
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:17,400
It's just not fair because some of the guys are so much taller than the others.

8
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:20,940
What? What? Something's wrong.

9
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:22,860
Our train's leaving on time.

10
00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:24,340
Have a new pellet.

11
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:25,040
It's care to taste your luck?

12
00:00:25,240 --> 00:00:26,040
Don't I get you two?

13
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:28,380
Hey, all you gotta do is find the queen.

14
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:34,100
Barney? Abe! Oh, Abe, I've been worried sick about you.

15
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:34,700
Where you been?

16
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:35,460
Oh, I'm fine.

17
00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:36,980
I've been living with a family in the suburbs.

18
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:37,980
Oh, that's great.

19
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:39,360
Have they found out yet?

20
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:42,100
Excuse me, I'm Jason Siever.

21
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:43,360
Oh, Barney Papadakis.

22
00:00:44,140 --> 00:00:45,360
Boy, you clean up good.

23
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:49,200
You're blonde. You look good too, Barney.

24
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:51,240
Yeah, well, it's this new exercise program.

25
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:53,780
Every time I see a transit cop, I run a quarter a mile.

26
00:00:55,140 --> 00:00:56,000
Speaking of which,

27
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:59,560
Well, sir Anderson, love the outfit.

28
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:01,320
Blue is your color.

29
00:01:02,360 --> 00:01:06,100
Now, Barney... You wouldn't be running any kind of illegal gambling here now, would

30
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:08,900
you? No, see, actually, I was playing 52 -card pick -up.

31
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:10,940
With three cards?

32
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:12,320
I'm not very good.

33
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:14,020
Beat it, Barney.

34
00:01:14,460 --> 00:01:16,840
Gotcha. Well, so long, fellas.

35
00:01:17,080 --> 00:01:20,020
I got enough quarters to go to the bus station and watch the whole molly dodd.

36
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:22,760
Nice seeing you, Abe.

37
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:24,160
See you, Barney.

38
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,740
So, uh, you know this guy, huh?

39
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:32,720
Everybody on the street knows Barney.

40
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:35,460
If you need a place to sleep and some food, Barney's the guy to see.

41
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:39,240
And if he's good, I guess, uh, he can do all right running a card scam.

42
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:40,740
It's not a scam.

43
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:43,940
Barney is New York's only honest three -card multiplayer.

44
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:46,080
He's got faster hands than Warren Beatty.

45
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:49,000
Why does he call you Abe?

46
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:52,480
Well, it's because my birthday is the same as Lincoln's.

47
00:01:53,260 --> 00:01:54,140
Your birthday's the same as Lincoln's?

48
00:01:54,140 --> 00:01:58,660
December 25th? February 12th.

49
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:01,900
Right. Wait, wait a minute, your birthday is next week?

50
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:03,260
You didn't tell us about it?

51
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:05,280
What are you, nuts?

52
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:08,040
How do you expect to get any good presents?

53
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:10,340
Hey, I don't need any presents.

54
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:13,200
Oh, Luke. He's just kidding, Dad.

55
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:16,180
Actually, he wants a leather jacket of my size.

56
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:19,440
Show me that smile.

57
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:21,760
Oh, show me that smile.

58
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:25,640
Don't waste another minute on your crying.

59
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:27,620
We're nowhere near, near,

60
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:32,180
nowhere near, the best season to begin.

61
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:38,640
Oh, as long as we got each other,

62
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:48,200
we got the world spinning right in our hands, You and me, we gotta be the luckiest

63
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:50,940
dreamers who never quit dreaming.

64
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:54,040
As long as we keep on giving,

65
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:58,520
we can take anything that comes our way.

66
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:07,460
Baby, rain or shine, all the time, we got each other sharing the laughter and love.

67
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:39,660
oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh Now, that's a party.

68
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:45,620
The secret to a great party is who isn't there when you have it.

69
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:48,440
Huh? You know, a more private gathering.

70
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:50,800
You mean, just you and me?

71
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:56,840
What Ben is trying to say is that you should wait until Jason and I are out and have

72
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:58,840
a wild party here at the house.

73
00:03:59,460 --> 00:04:00,880
Or we could do that.

74
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:03,900
Forget it. Hey, it was Mom's idea.

75
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:06,320
Okay, okay, okay, I give up.

76
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:07,520
You can throw me a party.

77
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:10,860
All right. But please, don't make it a big deal, all right?

78
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:11,780
How can we?

79
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:13,260
Mom and Dad insist on being there.

80
00:04:14,360 --> 00:04:18,260
Okay, the party's gonna be a dud, but you can still make out like a bandit on the

81
00:04:18,260 --> 00:04:19,780
gifts. But I don't want any gifts.

82
00:04:20,040 --> 00:04:21,520
Would you quit thinking to yourself?

83
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:25,220
Okay, Chrissy, time for...

84
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:30,560
Chrissy, were you playing in my jewelry box again after I told you not to?

85
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:32,120
Why would you think that?

86
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:35,240
Because Mr. Blowhole was wearing my good pearl earrings.

87
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:39,200
Bad will, bad will!

88
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:44,840
You know, guys, I'm gonna make this the best birthday party ever.

89
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:46,020
I've got an idea.

90
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:49,280
This idea is so great, it even scares me.

91
00:04:51,020 --> 00:04:53,300
Last time you said that, I lost my eyebrows.

92
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:57,960
Hi, Dwight. Hey.

93
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:00,960
Here's the keys to the wagon.

94
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:04,540
Did Carol's plane get off safely?

95
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:06,880
Unfortunately, yes.

96
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:13,440
Don't get me wrong, I'm glad she's alive and all, but what the heck am I gonna do

97
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:16,500
for a month while she's away doing research at the British Museum?

98
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:18,500
Oh, don't take it so hard, Dwight.

99
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:20,820
She's gonna be back before you know it.

100
00:05:20,820 --> 00:05:22,840
Which reminds me, her plane left six hours ago.

101
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:23,360
Where you been?

102
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:26,820
Well, I stood there and I watched the vapor trail from her plane evaporate.

103
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:33,040
For six hours?

104
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:34,880
No, for five minutes.

105
00:05:35,820 --> 00:05:38,620
And then I did what any red -blooded American man would do.

106
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:41,220
Boyd, have you been drinking?

107
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:45,180
Well, I'd be a liar if I didn't confess.

108
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:48,640
I went down and I knocked back a few yuhus at the Bleecker Street Cinema.

109
00:05:50,220 --> 00:05:51,420
You went to the movies?

110
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:54,800
And I saw the avant -garde film festival Winter Death of an Avocado.

111
00:05:56,620 --> 00:05:57,840
Let me just guess here.

112
00:05:57,840 --> 00:05:59,760
I bet the ending had something to do with guacamole.

113
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:06,900
We see this blue lawn chair, and this old man comes, and he sits down on it.

114
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:10,520
He plays the xylophone, and then he dies.

115
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:18,700
That's it? It's a statement about man's tautological search for metaphysical

116
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:22,040
comfort. Where's the avocado?

117
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:23,920
There is no avocado.

118
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:27,740
If it's called death of an avocado, shouldn't there be an avocado in there

119
00:06:27,740 --> 00:06:32,000
somewhere? Well, that would be a little bit obvious, don't you think?

120
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:36,860
My Little Pony?

121
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:39,280
Is this the banner Mike wanted us to get?

122
00:06:39,720 --> 00:06:40,880
It's Luke's favorite.

123
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:44,100
Are you sure?

124
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:46,080
It's someone's favorite.

125
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:51,480
Hey, you know, I don't know why more people don't shop down at Mort's Party

126
00:06:51,480 --> 00:06:53,940
warehouse. They get some great deals down there.

127
00:06:53,940 --> 00:06:54,620
Look at this.

128
00:06:59,740 --> 00:07:01,060
What's that supposed to be?

129
00:07:01,640 --> 00:07:03,000
It's Pin the Tail on the Hamster.

130
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:07,680
I got it for free with the purchase of 20 party hats.

131
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:10,260
Jason, this is Greek.

132
00:07:10,360 --> 00:07:11,460
Does it say happy birthday?

133
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:13,020
Well, the salesman wasn't sure.

134
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:15,080
It's either that or Dukakis in 88.

135
00:07:16,880 --> 00:07:20,140
Oh, honey, I'm sorry to insinuate that you were cheap.

136
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:21,580
All right, apology accepted.

137
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:23,320
Now, where's Benny?

138
00:07:23,320 --> 00:07:25,200
supposed to help me find last year's candles.

139
00:07:25,260 --> 00:07:28,260
Oh, me? He and Dwight went to get more ice cream.

140
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:29,720
Dwight's here again?

141
00:07:29,920 --> 00:07:32,600
Well, honey, he misses Carol, and he's lonely.

142
00:07:32,840 --> 00:07:35,520
Yeah, but it seems like last couple of days, every time I turn around, there's

143
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:37,660
Dwight. Jason, you're exaggerating.

144
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:40,860
Last night, he was reading the newspaper over my shoulder.

145
00:07:41,280 --> 00:07:42,300
In the bathroom.

146
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:45,320
Leave him alone.

147
00:07:45,380 --> 00:07:47,340
Besides, he and Ben are really starting to get along.

148
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:53,980
See, you see, the lawn chair symbolizes the ontological conundrum that is this very

149
00:07:53,980 --> 00:07:56,820
existence. That's great, Dwight.

150
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:02,420
I never thought I'd hear myself say these words, but get Carol back now.

151
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:06,900
Dr. Siever, I got licorice, pineapple, crunch ice cream.

152
00:08:07,100 --> 00:08:08,720
If that's not too much of a cliché.

153
00:08:10,180 --> 00:08:13,160
Well, maybe we can find something exotic to put on top.

154
00:08:13,340 --> 00:08:15,160
Oh, I'm way ahead of you.

155
00:08:16,140 --> 00:08:19,880
Creamed corn. I'm sorry.

156
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:20,760
Get down here!

157
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:22,000
Everybody else, come here!

158
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:22,940
Come here, quick!

159
00:08:23,060 --> 00:08:25,040
Oh, you got cream corn, you got a party.

160
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:27,840
Look, you ready for your birthday present?

161
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:37,500
Hi, Daddy! Barney showed me how to do a card trick.

162
00:08:37,700 --> 00:08:39,420
Oh, great, sweetie, let's see.

163
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:40,700
Find the queen.

164
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:41,760
Where's she hiding?

165
00:08:42,260 --> 00:08:44,060
For your liquid date, buddy.

166
00:08:46,940 --> 00:08:48,980
Come on, kiddo, maybe we should talk to him a little later.

167
00:08:49,220 --> 00:08:50,920
No, come on, Barney, show him how you do it.

168
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:53,760
No. He's a lot better than I am.

169
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:55,960
Oh, come on, Barney, let's do this.

170
00:08:56,560 --> 00:09:01,180
Well, okay. Find the queen.

171
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:02,420
Where is she hiding?

172
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:04,160
Tower of London, Buckingham Palace,

173
00:09:04,820 --> 00:09:10,660
Motel 6. No offense, Barney, but you gotta get up pretty early in the morning to

174
00:09:10,660 --> 00:09:14,300
fool Benseep. How'd you do that?

175
00:09:16,140 --> 00:09:18,900
Well, I tell you, this is a lot better than your last birthday, huh, Abe?

176
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:20,580
Why, what happened?

177
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:24,540
Barney and some of the guys snuck me into the zoo after hours.

178
00:09:24,900 --> 00:09:26,360
Boy, did we eat well that night.

179
00:09:26,780 --> 00:09:27,620
What did you eat?

180
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:30,460
Hot dogs. The dumpster was full of them.

181
00:09:35,620 --> 00:09:37,600
No matter what, you always helped us out.

182
00:09:37,700 --> 00:09:39,840
You could always get us a place to sleep and some food.

183
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:41,720
It's a gift.

184
00:09:43,820 --> 00:09:46,500
Sounds like Luke was pretty lucky to have you looking out for him.

185
00:09:47,480 --> 00:09:50,860
Yeah, well, you're gonna have to excuse me.

186
00:09:51,220 --> 00:09:53,020
I have to answer nature's call.

187
00:09:53,740 --> 00:09:57,720
Oh, Barney, why don't you just go upstairs, second door on the right.

188
00:09:59,080 --> 00:09:59,900
Of course I have it.

189
00:10:01,620 --> 00:10:03,480
What do you say we play some games?

190
00:10:03,720 --> 00:10:04,560
Sure. Yeah, all right.

191
00:10:04,660 --> 00:10:06,020
I have a great one.

192
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:07,520
What, spin the doofus?

193
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:15,780
Maybe later. This is called semantics.

194
00:10:16,380 --> 00:10:20,140
Someone names a word, and then we all try to give it subtle shades of meaning.

195
00:10:22,600 --> 00:10:23,620
Stay back, ladies.

196
00:10:23,820 --> 00:10:24,660
Carol saw him first.

197
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:29,920
Dwight, that's, uh, sounds like the kind of game that usually ends a party.

198
00:10:30,360 --> 00:10:31,580
I've noticed that.

199
00:10:32,740 --> 00:10:36,380
Maybe we can build up to that, but, uh, how about a rousing game of pin the tail on

200
00:10:36,380 --> 00:10:38,720
the hamster? Where's the camera?

201
00:10:38,740 --> 00:10:39,520
I want to get a picture of this.

202
00:10:39,640 --> 00:10:40,660
Upstairs, hall closet.

203
00:10:40,660 --> 00:10:41,640
Oh, I'll get it.

204
00:10:41,880 --> 00:10:44,860
Um, I just have one question about this hamster thing.

205
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:47,420
How do we hold the little guy down?

206
00:10:58,760 --> 00:10:59,760
What were you doing?

207
00:11:00,140 --> 00:11:02,040
Oh, just seeing how the other half lives.

208
00:11:03,140 --> 00:11:05,620
You know, this statue thing pulls this whole room together.

209
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:07,440
What were you doing in Mrs.

210
00:11:07,640 --> 00:11:08,600
Heaver's jewelry box?

211
00:11:10,140 --> 00:11:11,780
Barney, were you gonna take something?

212
00:11:12,560 --> 00:11:14,980
Hey, how could you say something like that?

213
00:11:16,140 --> 00:11:19,540
Well, I... I was on my way to the bathroom, and the door was open.

214
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:24,360
I know I shouldn't have come in here, but I saw the jewelry box on the dresser.

215
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:26,580
I used to look through my mom's all the time.

216
00:11:27,620 --> 00:11:31,440
Hers had this little dancing ballerina in it.

217
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:35,600
And I just wanted to see if this one had one, too.

218
00:11:38,040 --> 00:11:40,940
Oh. Um, Barney, I'm sorry.

219
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:43,500
You know, I shouldn't have jumped to a conclusion like that.

220
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:46,240
Hey, you forgive me?

221
00:11:46,720 --> 00:11:49,600
Yeah, sure. Come on, let's go back downstairs.

222
00:11:49,720 --> 00:11:54,040
Yeah. By the way, what's with the Dukakis hats?

223
00:11:55,860 --> 00:11:57,940
You're warm. Yeah, you're really, really warm.

224
00:11:58,680 --> 00:12:00,960
You're gorgeous. Dwight!

225
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:03,240
Dwight! Any minute, Mrs.

226
00:12:03,540 --> 00:12:05,480
Steven. No, no, no.

227
00:12:06,260 --> 00:12:07,500
Oh, I can't leave now.

228
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:11,680
I'm winning. Hey, uh, look, what'd he say you opened up your presents, huh?

229
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:14,120
This one's from me and Carol.

230
00:12:18,780 --> 00:12:21,140
The complete works of Schopenhauer.

231
00:12:22,420 --> 00:12:23,320
I knew it.

232
00:12:23,320 --> 00:12:24,260
You already read it.

233
00:12:25,740 --> 00:12:27,800
Hey, but not in its original German.

234
00:12:28,940 --> 00:12:30,180
All right, now mine.

235
00:12:36,300 --> 00:12:39,140
Mega Slime, Hammer and Sickle, The Nurses?

236
00:12:39,380 --> 00:12:41,720
Yeah, it's got their hit single, Turn Your Head and Cough.

237
00:12:43,840 --> 00:12:45,320
Can I borrow your Walkman sometime?

238
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:47,680
Sure. Hey, maybe you ought to open ours next.

239
00:12:49,560 --> 00:12:51,120
Hey, my own Walkman!

240
00:12:51,520 --> 00:12:53,340
Thanks! Yeah, you're welcome.

241
00:12:53,740 --> 00:12:55,700
Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh!

242
00:12:56,080 --> 00:12:59,360
Ooh. Are those the pearls I gave you for our anniversary?

243
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:01,600
No, honey, they're the real ones.

244
00:13:04,820 --> 00:13:09,480
Someone broke them and glued them back together with rubber cement.

245
00:13:10,160 --> 00:13:12,660
Uh, open mine next.

246
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:15,280
I'll be right back.

247
00:13:18,540 --> 00:13:20,640
Oh. It's the whole family.

248
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:22,620
That's you. Oh.

249
00:13:23,320 --> 00:13:27,100
Oh. Thanks. It's the best present I've ever gotten.

250
00:13:28,040 --> 00:13:29,320
Will you put it on your wall?

251
00:13:29,680 --> 00:13:30,680
I'll do it right now.

252
00:13:30,700 --> 00:13:32,500
